      "text": "We are given that \\(\\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\) is a root of the quadratic equation \\(5x^2 + 21x + v = 0\\). Let's denote the root by \\(x_1 = \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\). By Vieta's formulas, the sum and product of the roots of the quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) are given by \\(-\\frac{b}{a}\\) and \\(\\frac{c}{a}\\) respectively.\n\nFor the quadratic equation \\(5x^2 + 21x + v = 0\\), the sum of the roots is given by \\(-\\frac{21}{5}\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\nx_1 + x_2 = -\\frac{21}{5}\n\\]\nwhere \\(x_2\\) is the other root. Substituting \\(x_1 = \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} + x_2 = -\\frac{21}{5} = -\\frac{42}{10} = -\\frac{21}{5}\n\\]\nTo find \\(x_2\\), we solve for \\(x_2\\):\n\\[\nx_2 = -\\frac{21}{5} - \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} = -\\frac{21}{5} + \\frac{21+\\sqrt{301}}{10}\n\\]\nTo combine the fractions, we find a common denominator:\n\\[\nx_2 = \\frac{-42}{10} + \\frac{21+\\sqrt{301}}{10} = \\frac{-42 + 21 + \\sqrt{301}}{10} = \\frac{-21 + \\sqrt{301}}{10}\n\\]\nSo, the other root \\(x_2\\) is \\(\\frac{-21+\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\).\n\nNext, we use the product of the roots, which is given by \\(\\frac{v}{5}\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\nx_1 \\cdot x_2 = \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} \\cdot \\frac{-21+\\sqrt{301}}{10} = \\frac{(-21)^2 - (\\sqrt{301})^2}{100} = \\frac{441 - 301}{100} = \\frac{140}{100} = \\frac{7}{5}\n\\]\nSince \\(x_1 \\cdot x_2 = \\frac{v}{5}\\), we substitute the value of \\(x_1 \\cdot x_2\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{v}{5} = \\frac{7}{5}\n\\]\nSolving for \\(v\\), we get:\n\\[\nv = 7\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\(v\\) is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).",
